To create a Rust application that supports the Eleven Labs API and reads the contents of the clipboard aloud using Text-to-Speech (TTS), you can follow these steps:

1. Install Rust:
   Follow the instructions on the official Rust website to install Rust: https://www.rust-lang.org/tools/install

2. Create a new Rust project:
   Open a terminal and run the following command to create a new Rust project:

   ```
   cargo new elevenlabs_tts --bin
   ```

3. Add dependencies:
   Open the `Cargo.toml` file in the project directory and add the following dependencies:

   ```toml
   [dependencies]
   reqwest = { version = "0.11", features = ["json"] }
   serde = { version = "1.0", features = ["derive"] }
   serde_json = "1.0"
   clipboard = "3.0"
   text-to-speech = "0.11.0"
   ```

   Save and close the file.

4. Implement the main function:
   Open the `src/main.rs` file and replace its content with the following code:

   ```rust
   use reqwest::Client;
   use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};
   use clipboard::ClipboardProvider;
   use text_to_speech::{Speaker, SpeechSynthesizer};

   #[derive(Serialize, Deserialize)]
   struct Response {
       text: String,
   }

   async fn get_api_response() -> Result<Response, reqwest::Error> {
       let client = Client::new();
       let response = client
           .get("https://api.elevenlabs.io/v1/text-to-speech")
           .header("Authorization", "Bearer YOUR_API_KEY")
           .send()
           .await?
           .json::<Response>()
           .await?;

       Ok(response)
   }

   async fn speak(text: &str) {
       let mut speaker = Speaker::new().unwrap();
       let synthesizer = SpeechSynthesizer::new().unwrap();
       let speech = synthesizer.synthesize(text).unwrap();
       speaker.speak(speech).unwrap();
   }

   async fn main() {
       let api_response = get_api_response().await.unwrap();
       let clipboard_content = ClipboardProvider::new().get_contents().unwrap();

       let combined_text = format!("{} {}", api_response.text, clipboard_content);
       speak(&combined_text).await;
   }

   fn main() {
       tokio::runtime::Builder::new_multi_thread()
           .build_async()
           .expect("Failed to create a tokio runtime")
           .block_on(main)
   }
   ```

   Replace `YOUR_API_KEY` with your Eleven Labs API key.

5. Build and run the application:
   Run the following command to build the application:

   ```
   cargo build --release
   ```

   After the build process is complete, you can run the application with:

   ```
   ./target/release/elevenlabs_tts
   ```

   The application will read the combined text from the Eleven Labs API and the clipboard aloud using TTS.

