Here's a solution for packing and unpacking an array of entities into an ArrayBuffer. The entities have the fields `id` (4 bits) and `size` (3 bits).

```javascript
function packEntities(entities) {
  const buffer = new ArrayBuffer(entities.length);
  const view = new DataView(buffer);

  for (let i = 0; i < entities.length; i++) {
    const entity = entities[i];
    const id = entity.id & 0xF; // Masking to get the lower 4 bits
    const size = entity.size & 0x7; // Masking to get the lower 3 bits

    view.setUint8(i * (id + size), id);
    view.setUint8(i * (id + size) + 1, size);

    // Set the remaining bits of the byte for the id and size fields
    view.setUint8(
      i * (id + size) + 2,
      (entity.id >> 4) | (entity.size << 3)
    );
  }

  return buffer;
}

function unpackEntities(buffer, length) {
  const entities = [];
  const view = new DataView(buffer);

  for (let i = 0; i < length; i++) {
    const idByte = view.getUint8(i);
    const sizeByte = view.getUint8(i + 1);
    const remainingByte = view.getUint8(i + 2);

    const id = (idByte << 4) | (remainingByte >> 3);
    const size = remainingByte & 0x7;

    entities.push({ id, size });

    // Move to the next entity
    i += id + size;
  }

  return entities;
}
```

In the `packEntities` function, we create an ArrayBuffer and a DataView to manipulate the buffer. We iterate through the entities and pack each one into the buffer. The id and size are stored in three consecutive bytes, with the id taking up the lower 4 bits, the size taking up the lower 3 bits, and the remaining bits being used to store the upper 4 bits of the id and the remaining 3 bits of the size.

In the `unpackEntities` function, we create an ArrayBuffer and a DataView to manipulate the buffer. We iterate through the buffer and unpack each entity. The id and size are extracted from the three consecutive bytes, and the remaining entity objects are pushed into an array. The function returns the array of entities.

